1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,800
Pactshift was built by Shivam Gupta.

2
00:00:03,720 --> 00:00:05,480
An agency agrees to build a website.

3
00:00:06,440 --> 00:00:09,120
Halfway through, the client asks for one
more thing.

4
00:00:09,880 --> 00:00:13,500
The request changes, but the budget and
delivery plan often don't.

5
00:00:14,580 --> 00:00:17,840
Pactshift turns that conversation into a
choice everyone can

6
00:00:17,840 --> 00:00:20,840
see, and an agreement the project actually
follows.

7
00:00:21,481 --> 00:00:24,761
Let's take a real workflow using fictional
sample data.

8
00:00:25,741 --> 00:00:28,641
Northstar Studio is building this website
for Forma Architecture.

9
00:00:29,521 --> 00:00:31,161
The fixed budget is twelve thousand
dollars.

10
00:00:32,221 --> 00:00:34,781
The agreed work totals ninety-six
estimated hours.

11
00:00:35,961 --> 00:00:39,301
Now the client wants Spanish-language
pages, estimated at twelve

12
00:00:39,301 --> 00:00:42,161
hours, with the translated copy supplied
by the client.

13
00:00:42,696 --> 00:00:44,836
The request sits beside the saved scope.

14
00:00:45,456 --> 00:00:47,636
The analysis helps the owner review what
is changing,

15
00:00:47,776 --> 00:00:50,376
and the interface shows which engine
produced it.

16
00:00:51,016 --> 00:00:52,896
AI can assist here, but it does

17
00:00:52,896 --> 00:00:54,536
not decide what the client owes.

18
00:00:55,196 --> 00:00:56,776
The owner confirms the effort.

19
00:00:57,516 --> 00:01:00,936
The server calculates the fee from the
project's rate: twelve hours

20
00:01:01,236 --> 00:01:04,076
at one hundred and twenty-five dollars is
fifteen hundred dollars.

21
00:01:05,050 --> 00:01:06,810
Charging more is one option.

22
00:01:07,510 --> 00:01:08,910
But the client may care more about

23
00:01:08,910 --> 00:01:10,690
the launch date and a fixed budget.

24
00:01:11,210 --> 00:01:12,990
So the agency can offer an exchange.

25
00:01:13,790 --> 00:01:17,730
This resource library is planned for
sixteen hours and hasn't started.

26
00:01:18,450 --> 00:01:22,130
We can replace it with the twelve-hour
language addition.

27
00:01:22,130 --> 00:01:26,030
Work already underway, locked
deliverables, and dependencies constrain

28
00:01:26,030 --> 00:01:27,030
what can be offered.

29
00:01:27,767 --> 00:01:31,147
The agency shares the proposal and opens
the client view.

30
00:01:31,867 --> 00:01:33,827
The client doesn't need to create an
account.

31
00:01:34,447 --> 00:01:36,667
They can see the addition with its fee and
schedule

32
00:01:36,667 --> 00:01:40,627
impact, the exact deliverable exchange, or
the option to defer.

33
00:01:41,367 --> 00:01:42,527
They also see the assumptions.

34
00:01:43,507 --> 00:01:46,447
There is no surprise invoice hiding behind
an approval button.

35
00:01:47,075 --> 00:01:50,415
In this demonstration, the fictional
client chooses the exchange.

36
00:01:51,175 --> 00:01:54,575
They enter a name, acknowledge the terms,
and confirm the choice.

37
00:01:55,506 --> 00:01:58,386
Back in the agency workspace, this is

38
00:01:58,386 --> 00:02:00,406
the important part: the agreement changed.

39
00:02:00,986 --> 00:02:04,346
The resource library has been exchanged
for the Spanish-language pages.

40
00:02:04,926 --> 00:02:06,846
The budget is still twelve thousand
dollars.

41
00:02:07,566 --> 00:02:09,246
The delivery date is unchanged.

42
00:02:10,206 --> 00:02:13,006
Total estimated work moves from ninety-six
to ninety-two

43
00:02:13,006 --> 00:02:15,746
hours, and the new baseline records the
decision.

44
00:02:16,572 --> 00:02:18,912
That is four hours of planned capacity

45
00:02:18,912 --> 00:02:21,212
preserved, not four hours of revenue.

46
00:02:21,972 --> 00:02:25,752
The system also keeps previous baselines
and a linked decision history.

47
00:02:26,892 --> 00:02:29,272
Version checks protect against stale
approvals, and

48
00:02:29,272 --> 00:02:31,692
repeated acceptance cannot duplicate the
new work.

49
00:02:32,392 --> 00:02:34,592
The owner can export the workspace record.

50
00:02:35,372 --> 00:02:38,172
The client acknowledgement records a
decision; it

51
00:02:38,172 --> 00:02:40,572
does not independently verify the signer's
identity.

52
00:02:41,451 --> 00:02:44,651
Pactshift is built for small agencies that
need

53
00:02:44,651 --> 00:02:46,431
this conversation handled clearly.

54
00:02:47,291 --> 00:02:51,311
The proposed subscriptions start with a
free plan, then twenty-nine dollars for

55
00:02:51,311 --> 00:02:55,071
Studio and seventy-nine dollars for
Agency, with defined

56
00:02:55,071 --> 00:02:57,311
stored-project and analysis allowances.

57
00:02:58,411 --> 00:03:00,971
Client review has no seat charge, and
Pactshift

58
00:03:00,971 --> 00:03:03,531
takes no cut of an agency's approved work.

59
00:03:04,008 --> 00:03:07,828
This is a pre-validation product, with no
claimed customers or revenue.

60
00:03:08,668 --> 00:03:12,228
Shivam developed Pactshift with
substantial AI assistance, and the next

61
00:03:12,228 --> 00:03:15,488
step is testing this workflow with real
agency owners and their clients.

62
00:03:16,128 --> 00:03:18,888
The goal is simple: when the request
changes, help

63
00:03:18,888 --> 00:03:20,748
both sides agree on what changes with it.
